1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:02,590
How do you find
an extinct giant

2
00:00:02,590 --> 00:00:04,450
without finding its body?

3
00:00:04,970 --> 00:00:06,350
In northern Greenland,

4
00:00:06,380 --> 00:00:09,480
scientists found traces
of mastodons

5
00:00:09,640 --> 00:00:11,830
in sediment about
two million years old.

6
00:00:12,460 --> 00:00:13,900
The clue wasn’t a skeleton.

7
00:00:14,500 --> 00:00:16,450
It was broken DNA.

8
00:00:16,990 --> 00:00:19,850
Living things leave
genetic traces behind.

9
00:00:20,190 --> 00:00:22,500
Some fragments can
bind to minerals,

10
00:00:22,770 --> 00:00:24,000
helping them survive.

11
00:00:24,430 --> 00:00:26,900
Scientists compared
the damaged pieces

12
00:00:27,260 --> 00:00:28,500
with known DNA.

13
00:00:28,940 --> 00:00:30,650
Together with plant DNA,

14
00:00:30,780 --> 00:00:33,950
they revealed evidence of
a vanished forest ecosystem.

15
00:00:34,520 --> 00:00:36,440
Not a resurrected animal.

16
00:00:36,700 --> 00:00:38,640
Not a complete genome.

17
00:00:38,920 --> 00:00:40,650
But here’s the harder mystery:

18
00:00:40,970 --> 00:00:43,390
how do you know
those tiny pieces

19
00:00:43,750 --> 00:00:45,650
belong to a mastodon?

20
00:00:45,930 --> 00:00:47,820
Follow the full investigation

21
00:00:48,140 --> 00:00:49,240
in the linked video.
